.TH SNIPROXY 8 "23 Sep 2026" "SNIProxy manual" "sniproxy"

.SH NAME

SNIProxy \- transparent session based TLS, HTTP, DTLS, XMPP, and Minecraft proxy

.SH SYNOPSIS

\fBsniproxy\fR [ -\fBc\fR \fIconfig\fR ] [ -\fBf\fR ] [ -\fBg\fR ] [ -\fBt\fR ]
[ -\fBn\fR \fIfile-descriptor-limit\fR ] [ -\fBV\fR ] [ -\fBT\fR \fImin-tls-version\fR ] [ -\fBd\fR ]

.SH DESCRIPTION

Proxies incoming HTTP, TLS, DTLS, XMPP, and Minecraft Java Edition
connections, including HTTP/2 requests, based on the hostname contained in
the initial request. For TCP protocols (TLS, HTTP, XMPP, Minecraft) the
hostname is extracted from the initial TCP stream. For DTLS the hostname is
extracted from the SNI extension in the UDP ClientHello datagram, enabling
proxying by hostname without decryption of DTLS protocols whose clients
send SNI, such as CoAP. This enables HTTPS name-based virtual hosting
to separate backend servers without installing the private key on the proxy
machine\&. By default TLS 1.2 (and newer) client hellos are
accepted\&.

For security reasons the configuration file must grant no permission to
other users, and none to its group unless
.B -g
allows group read\&. sniproxy verifies the permissions after
opening the file and aborts startup if it detects broader access; a
reload with such a file is refused and keeps the running configuration\&.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
-c \fIconfig\fR
Specify configuration file to use. The default is
/etc/sniproxy\&.conf\&.

.TP
-f
Do not daemonize, and run in foreground\&. The
.B pidfile
directive is then ignored: no pidfile is checked, written or
removed\&.

.TP
-g
Allow group-read (0640) permissions on the configuration file\&.
By default the mode may be at most 0600\&. This flag relaxes the check
to at most 0640 so the unprivileged process can re-read the config on
SIGHUP reload\&. The config file should be owned by root with its group set to
the sniproxy user group (e\&.g\&. root:_sniproxy)\&. A security warning is
logged at startup when this flag is used\&.

.TP
-t
Test configuration and exit\&. Parses the configuration file and reports
whether it is valid without starting the proxy\&. The log files it names
are opened as they would be at startup, so missing ones are created\&.

.TP
-n \fIfile-descriptor-limit\fR
Set both the soft and the hard file descriptor limit to this value
before privileges are dropped\&. The default is 65536, which is also
applied without this option and can lower a higher inherited limit\&.
If the limit cannot be set sniproxy logs a warning and goes on; the
default connection limit (see
.B max_connections
in
.BR sniproxy.conf (5))
is still derived from this value\&.

.TP
-V
Print the version of SNIProxy and exit\&.

.TP
-T \fImin-tls-version\fR
Set the minimum TLS ClientHello version that will be accepted\&. The
value must be one of 1.0, 1.1, 1.2, or 1.3\&. For 1.0 to 1.2 the legacy
version field of the ClientHello is checked; for 1.3 that field is
ignored, since TLS 1.3 clients set it to 1.2, and the supported_versions
extension must list 1.3 or later\&. The default is 1.2\&. It applies to
TLS listeners only, not to DTLS\&.

.TP
-d
Enable verbose resolver debug logging to aid DNS troubleshooting\&. The
messages go to the error log at notice priority, so an error log set
to warning or stricter drops them\&. When the error log is a file the resolver
process sends them to syslog instead, see the ERROR_LOG section of
\fBsniproxy.conf\fR(5)\&.

.SH SIGNALS

.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reopen log files and reload the configuration file\&. Connections
that are already routed keep their backend; the others, and new ones,
use the updated configuration\&. Changes to user, group and pidfile,
to the resolver's nameserver, search, mode and dnssec_validation, and
to tcp_fastopen, reuseport and ipv6_v6only for listeners that already
exist, only take effect on restart; the reload logs a warning for each
one it ignores\&. The resolver's query limits do change on reload\&.
When sniproxy was started as root with no listener using
.BR "source client" ,
one added by a reload also needs a restart, and the reload warns about
it\&.

.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Dump all active TCP connections and UDP sessions to a new
.I connections-XXXXXX
file for debugging, and log its path at notice priority\&. The file is
created in
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sniproxy\fR,
.I /var/run/sniproxy\fR,
or
.I /tmp/sniproxy-<uid>
(tried in that order)\&.

.TP
.B SIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM
Shut down: every connection and UDP session is closed at once without
draining, the helper processes are stopped and the pidfile is
removed\&.

.SH SANDBOXING
On OpenBSD,
\fBsniproxy\fR
uses
\fBpledge\fR(2)
and
\fBunveil\fR(2)
to restrict each process to the system calls it needs and the files it
uses\&. The main process and the binder and resolver it forks see the
configuration, the resolver files, the certificate bundle, and the
directories holding the log files, the pidfile, listening Unix sockets
and connection dumps, in which they may create files\&. The logger process is forked before the
filesystem view is locked and unveils only its own log files once
privileges are dropped, so a log file first named by a reload is only
opened after a restart\&.
.PP
On FreeBSD,
\fBsniproxy\fR
runs the logger process in Capsicum capability mode
(\fBcap_enter\fR(2)),
with the directories it needs for log rotation pre-opened so that
\fBopenat\fR(2)
works there\&. The main and resolver processes stay out of capability
mode, which does not permit
\fBconnect\fR(2),
and limit the rights of their IPC descriptors with
\fBcap_rights_limit\fR(2)
instead; the binder is not confined\&.
Set
.B SNIPROXY_DISABLE_CAPSICUM
to any non-empty value in the environment to disable Capsicum
sandboxing\&.
.PP
On Linux,
\fBsniproxy\fR
uses seccomp-bpf to restrict available system calls per process when it
was built with libseccomp\&.
Set
.B SNIPROXY_DISABLE_SECCOMP
to any non-empty value in the environment to disable it\&.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sniproxy.conf
Default configuration file\&.
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/sniproxy.conf
Configuration file passed with
.B -c
by the FreeBSD rc.d script\&. The script also expects the configuration
to set
.B pidfile /var/run/sniproxy.pid\fR\&.
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/rc.d/sniproxy
FreeBSD rc.d startup script\&.
Enable with
.B sysrc sniproxy_enable=YES
and control with
.B service sniproxy start|stop|reload\fR\&.
.TP
.I /etc/init.d/sniproxy
On Alpine, the OpenRC service installed by the package\&.
Enable with
.B rc-update add sniproxy
and control with
.B rc-service sniproxy start|stop|reload\fR\&.
.TP
.I /etc/conf.d/sniproxy
Optional settings for the OpenRC service:
.B SNIPROXY_CONFIG
(default
.IR /etc/sniproxy.conf )
and
.B SNIPROXY_OPTS
(default
.BR -g )\&.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBsniproxy.conf\fR(5)\fR,
\fBrc.subr\fR(8)\fR,
\fBopenrc-run\fR(8)
